WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.941
One Redis runs every command on one thread, in one machine's memory.

2
00:00:06.941 --> 00:00:11.431
When the payment keys outgrow it, you split them across three primaries.

3
00:00:11.431 --> 00:00:13.301
So where does payment:42 live?

4
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:18.541
Hash the key mod three, and adding a fourth server later moves three keys in four.

5
00:00:18.767 --> 00:00:24.387
Redis Cluster puts a fixed layer in between: 16,384 hash slots.

6
00:00:24.387 --> 00:00:27.420
Each primary owns a range of them.

7
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:32.952
A key's slot is a checksum of the key, mod 16,384.

8
00:00:32.952 --> 00:00:38.484
payment:42 lands in slot 9,055, which node two owns.

9
00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:42.758
Send it to node one, and node one doesn't forward it.

10
00:00:42.758 --> 00:00:46.969
It answers MOVED: slot 9,055 is on node two, ask there.

11
00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:53.621
So a cluster client loads the slot table, works out each slot itself, and goes straight to the owner.

12
00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:57.393
Now the operator moves slot 9,055 to node three.

13
00:00:57.393 --> 00:01:03.157
Its keys copy across in batches, so for a while each node holds some of them.

14
00:01:03.367 --> 00:01:13.229
Ask node two for a key that already left, and it answers ASK: send just this command to node three, and keep your table, because the slot hasn't moved yet.

15
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:19.359
When the last key is across, node three owns the slot, and node two answers MOVED from then on.

16
00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:24.240
Commands that name several keys need them in one slot.

17
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:34.437
This MSET names slots 9,055 and 13,182, so Redis refuses it with CROSSSLOT, even though both sit on node three today.

18
00:01:34.567 --> 00:01:36.213
A hash tag fixes it.

19
00:01:36.213 --> 00:01:44.607
Only the part inside the braces is hashed, so both of a merchant's keys land in slot 10,890, together.

20
00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:51.655
A key hashes to a slot, a slot belongs to one node, and keys that must change together share a hash tag.
